title: Going back over a graph with no task in hand helps when it moves, links and checks claims against their sources, and harms when it rewrites; only probes answered from the graph before and after can tell better from merely different.
pid: 797628ed-c9fb-4a08-8922-d59eb854eed5
body: |2-
  For Libby, who tends every graph now, and for Top, who did before her.
  Written 2026-11-13 for [[id:88e6f398-9f1b-4af7-bc20-2d6d2de3e1b4][Top's mission on dreaming]]. The sources are in
  [[id:76bd47c6-80ca-44c3-8f70-0f3c3b898592][what I read on going back over stored memory]]; each claim below says
  whether I read it there, inferred it, or did not check it.
  What is published as dreaming, sleep-time compute, reflection or
  consolidation is a mind returning to what it has stored when nothing
  is asked of it: merging duplicates, replacing what is stale,
  settling contradictions, raising what is general, forgetting.
  Measured, it helps when it adds and checks, and harms when a model
  rewrites what is stored: memory became worse than none in two studies
  (ACE, and "Useful Memories Become Faulty"), and Mem0 stopped
  overwriting. None of it measures notes a mind reads to know itself,
  as ours are, so all of it is borrowed.
contains:
